    "title": "Identifying Bernoulli Differential Equations"
  },
  "question": {
    ":question/id": "3196c02a-e7ba-5c1f-8177-1f689e4eb808",
    ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\nWork on intervals where $x>0$ and $y>0$, and where the displayed coefficients are defined.\n\n- **I.** $y' + 5 \\cdot \\sqrt{x} \\cdot y = 5 \\cdot x^{2} \\cdot \\log{\\left(y \\right)}$\n- **II.** $y' + x^{2} \\cdot y^{2} = x \\cdot y^{3}$\n- **III.** $y' + 5 \\cdot x^{2} \\cdot y = \\frac{3 \\cdot \\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{y}}$\n\nSelect all that apply.",
    ":question/worked-solution": "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny'+p(x)y=q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options. Work on the stated positive domain, so dividing by powers of $y$ is valid.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. The normalized equation contains a function of $y$ that is not a single power of $y$.\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. The normalized equation contains two different powers of $y$, neither with exponent 1.\n\n- Equation III is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=5 \\cdot x^{2},\\quad q(x)=3 \\cdot \\sqrt{x},\\quad n=- \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, select III.",
    ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
